00:07:15,456 --> 00:07:15,696
Right.

110
00:07:15,936 --> 00:07:17,296
There is a very big catch.

111
00:07:17,582 --> 00:07:20,416
The theorem must only work under specific rigid conditions.

112
00:07:20,816 --> 00:07:24,292
Yes, the math completely breaks down if the data is structured incorrectly.

113
00:07:24,896 --> 00:07:29,572
Theorem one only holds true if the model is fed the right diet of data.

114
00:07:30,318 --> 00:07:38,020
Which brings us to a formal critical distinction, Balastriero and Lacoon make, between what they classify as good views and bad views.

115
00:07:38,702 --> 00:07:47,236
Okay, so if a bad view is just like a camera trick or a manipulation, a good view must require totally independent observations of the same event.

116
00:07:47,726 --> 00:07:49,504
Let's make that concrete for the listener.

117
00:07:49,904 --> 00:07:50,212
Sure.

118
00:07:50,574 --> 00:07:55,812
A good view consists of two conditionally independent observations of a single world scene.

119
00:07:56,462 --> 00:08:06,224
They must share that core physical reality, but their observational noise, the interference or the quirks from the medium itself, must be completely independent.

120
00:08:06,734 --> 00:08:08,784
So in computer vision, what does that look like?

121
00:08:09,184 --> 00:08:19,904
In vision, a good view would be two distinct photographs of the exact same physical scene, but taken from different camera angles or under entirely different lighting conditions.

122
00:08:20,224 --> 00:08:27,184
And for language, it would be two separate captions written by two completely independent human observers describing the exact same event.

123
00:08:27,504 --> 00:08:27,904
Precisely.

124
00:08:28,144 --> 00:08:31,744
Like two witnesses describing a car crash from opposite sides of the street.

125
00:08:32,046 --> 00:08:37,344
They are looking at the same reality, but using their own independent vocabulary, phrasing, and biases.

126
00:08:37,902 --> 00:08:38,371
Exactly.

127
00:08:38,544 --> 00:08:46,244
And because their phrasing is independent, the only shared information between those two captions is the physical reality of the car crash itself.

128
00:08:46,638 --> 00:08:50,944
The AI learns to isolate the crash and ignore the specific word choices.

129
00:08:51,264 --> 00:08:52,164
That makes total sense.

130
00:08:52,464 --> 00:08:55,076
Now, contrast that with a bad view.

131
00:08:55,598 --> 00:09:01,220
A bad view is an artificial corruption or a temporal continuation of a single observation.

132
00:09:01,614 --> 00:09:04,772
You aren't giving the model two independent looks at reality.

133
00:09:05,198 --> 00:09:08,772
You are giving it one single look and then playing a trick on it to test it.

134
00:09:09,184 --> 00:09:12,132
Wait, let me push back on this to make sure the boundary is totally clear.

135
00:09:12,430 --> 00:09:22,464
If I take a single photograph of my dog in my living room and I literally chop the image in half, and I ask the AI to predict the missing half based on the half it can see, that's a bad view.

136
00:09:22,926 --> 00:09:24,464
It is a fundamentally bad view, yes.

137
00:09:24,864 --> 00:09:32,932
Because the lighting, the camera sensor noise, the lens distortion, the exact angle, they are all absolutely identical on both halves.

138
00:09:33,264 --> 00:09:38,852
The AI is just learning the camera's quirks rather than learning the actual physical concept of a dog.

139
00:09:39,374 --> 00:09:42,948
Think about setting up a professional photography studio to test that exact scenario.

140
00:09:43,598 --> 00:09:53,184
If you have a single camera on a tripod and you slice its photo in half, or you digitally erase some of the pixels to hide them from the AI, that is a bad view.

141
00:09:53,518 --> 00:09:54,544
Because it's just one source.

142
00:09:54,864 --> 00:09:55,024
Right.

143
00:09:55,184 --> 00:09:57,796
The AI learns the artifact of that specific camera lens.

144
00:09:58,304 --> 00:10:06,820
But if you have two independent cameras in that studio, capturing the dog from entirely different angles at the exact same time, that is a good view.

145
00:10:07,374 --> 00:10:11,184
The only thing those two independent cameras share is the physical reality of the dog itself.

146
00:10:11,584 --> 00:10:16,544
Wow, this is a massive shift in how we should be thinking about feeding data to machines.

147
00:10:16,814 --> 00:10:24,944
And armed with this clear definition of bad views, we have to turn a highly critical eye toward the current state of the AI industry.

148
00:10:25,264 --> 00:10:25,744
We really do.

149
00:10:26,064 --> 00:10:36,464
Because almost the entire generative AI boom, like the chatbots you interact with daily, is built on something that Balestriero and Lacan mathematically prove is inherently flawed.

150
