The entire industry is obsessed with next token prediction.

151
00:10:40,224 --> 00:10:40,452
Right.

152
00:10:41,006 --> 00:10:53,700
Autoregressive next token prediction, where the AI looks at a sequence of words and tries to get the very next word in that exact same sequence, is mathematically classified in this paper as an archetypal bad view, Jepa.

153
00:10:54,064 --> 00:10:57,104
Because it's just a temporal continuation of a single observation.

154
00:10:57,184 --> 00:11:01,504
It's exactly like chopping the dog photo in half, but with a sentence instead of an image.

155
00:11:01,664 --> 00:11:07,104
You are taking a single paragraph, chopping the last word off, and saying, guess what goes here?

156
00:11:07,504 --> 00:11:08,004
Exactly.

157
00:11:08,304 --> 00:11:12,036
And the authors map this out with a strict mathematical misalignment bound.

158
00:11:12,462 --> 00:11:16,544
Essentially, the math proves that the AI's error rate is trapped.

159
00:11:17,006 --> 00:11:17,824
Trapped how?

160
00:11:18,144 --> 00:11:24,644
It is fundamentally bottlenecked by how much useless repetitive noise is shared between the prefix of the sentence and the missing word.

161
00:11:25,422 --> 00:11:30,340
Mathematically, the error is strictly bounded by the mutual information of modality nuisance variables.

162
00:11:30,784 --> 00:11:32,064
Nuisance variables.

163
00:11:32,464 --> 00:11:32,704
Right.

164
00:11:32,864 --> 00:11:45,060
Because the AI is predicting the immediate next word of a single continuous text, it is forced to waste massive parameter capacity modeling things like grammatical syntax, verb tenses, commas, and formatting conventions.

165
00:11:45,744 --> 00:11:51,984
So the nuisance variables are just the rules of language that dictate how we speak, but don't actually describe the physical laws of the world?

166
00:11:52,304 --> 00:11:52,772
Exactly.

167
00:11:53,166 --> 00:11:57,636
Those grammatical variables carry immense predictive weight between the start of a sentence and the next word.

168
00:11:57,966 --> 00:12:03,424
If I say the dog ran, the grammar strongly dictates the next word will be something like to or away.

169
00:12:03,758 --> 00:12:08,384
But those rules tell the model absolutely nothing about the actual physical reality of a dog running.

170
00:12:08,704 --> 00:12:09,252
Nothing at all.

171
00:12:09,614 --> 00:12:18,084
The model spends all its brain power learning that a comma usually follows a certain clause, rather than learning what the words actually represent in the physical world.

172
00:12:18,766 --> 00:12:20,640
Here's where it gets really interesting.

173
00:12:21,422 --> 00:12:25,120
I want you to think about how this changes your view of the AI you use every day.

