00:14:01,422 --> 00:14:03,920
We need the numbers here to really grasp the scale of this.

191
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:07,440
How much better is a good view compared to predicting the next word?

192
00:14:07,840 --> 00:14:15,920
With just 21.6 million training samples, the Goodview Text Jepa reached an alignment score of 0.109.

193
00:14:16,302 --> 00:14:17,920
Okay, 21.6 million.

194
00:14:18,254 --> 00:14:25,680
To reach that exact same score, a traditional next token prediction model required 900 million training samples.

195
00:14:26,126 --> 00:14:29,680
21.6 million compared to 900 million.

196
00:14:29,774 --> 00:14:32,640
That is a 42-fold sample efficiency advantage.

197
00:14:33,040 --> 00:14:35,040
It completely redefines the cost of training.

198
00:14:35,374 --> 00:14:40,400
To put that in perspective for you, imagine you're a research team trying to build a new model.

199
00:14:40,750 --> 00:14:53,040
A 42-fold advantage means a training run that would normally take over a month and cost hundreds of thousands of dollars on massive server farms could now be done in a single day for a fraction of the cost.

200
00:14:53,454 --> 00:14:53,892
Exactly.

201
00:14:54,446 --> 00:14:59,360
You are getting the exact same understanding of reality using 42 times less data.

202
00:14:59,854 --> 00:15:03,972
And that efficiency scales up to create an incredible David versus Goliath scenario.

203
